<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="act-eulerian-algorithm-steps">
  <title>Eulerian Circuit Algorithm Steps</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Suppose we have a connected graph with at least two vertices such that every vertex has even degree.  Here is the algorithm for constructing an Eulerian circuit.  Fill in justification for each step.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Choose an arbitrary vertex <m>v_0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Choose any edge incident to <m>v_0</m> that we have not yet traveled on and travel along it to another vertex <m>v_1</m> along this edge.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Keep repeating step 2 until we cannot do it anymore. At this point, we are back on vertex <m>v_0</m>.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Why will we eventually have to stop?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Why do we end up on the vertex <m>v_0</m>?  Hint:  Assume we are on any other vertex and find a contradiction.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        At this point, we have a circuit <m>v_0v_1 \cdots v_nv_0</m>. If we have traveled along each edge in the graph, we are done.  Why?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If we have some edges not yet traveled along, at least one of them is incident to one of <m>v_0$, $v_1, \ldots , v_n</m>.  Why?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        From #5, we now know that one of the vertices on our circuit is incident to an unused edge.  Relabel our vertices so that this one is <m>v_0</m> and our circuit is <m>v_0v_1 \cdots v_nv_0</m>.  Why is it safe to assume that <m>v_0</m> is the first vertex in the circuit?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Repeat steps 2-4 to create a new circuit <m>v_0w_1w_2 \cdots w_mv_0</m>.  Why will we end up at <m>v_0</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Take the circuits we created in step 3 and step 7 and turn them into a single circuit.  How is this done?  How do you know it is a circuit?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Repeat steps 4-8 until we are forced to stop.  Why are we eventually forced to stop?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        When we are forced to stop, we have created an Eulerian circuit.  Why?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>